      "publisher": "arXiv",
      "abstract": "We introduce a Mori-Zwanzig graph neural network (MZ-GNN) framework for learning reducedorder Lagrangian dynamics of tracer particles in homogeneous isotropic turbulence. The model represents particle acceleration as a finite-memory expansion over present and delayed particleneighborhood graphs, wit...",
      "year": 2026,
      "date": "2026-01-01",
      "citations": [
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.48550/arxiv.2606.14918",
          "targetDoi": "10.1146/annurev-fluid-030121-021103",
          "snippet": "Predicting the transport of material particles in turbulent flows remains a central challenge in fluid dynamics [1,2], with applications ranging from atmospheric pollutant and aerosol dispersion [3], droplet growth in warm clouds [4], and fuel-air mixing in combustion [5], to ocean mixing and climate dynamics [6]. Despite decades of progress, a first-principles understanding of turbulent transport remains elusive because turbulence is intrinsically multiscale, chaotic, and intermittent [7][8][9][10]: particle trajectories are shaped by velocity fluctuations spanning many decades, from the integral length scale down to the smallest dynamically active scales, the Kolmogorov length scales [11,12], with associated timescales ranging from the large-eddy turnover time to the fastest dissipative times [1,[13][14][15].",
          "section": "introduction",
          "type": "mentioning"
        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.48550/arxiv.2606.14918",
          "targetDoi": "10.1137/21m1401759",
          "snippet": "In the MZ-GNN, the regression is performed over the class of equivariant graph functions, so that the empirical projection is restricted to functions of the resolved particle graph that satisfy the required physical symmetries. The residual errors generated during learning play the role of the unresolved orthogonal dynamics, and the consistency of the learned memory terms is connected to the generalized fluctuation-dissipation relation [73,74].",
          "section": "the mori-zwanzig framework",
          "type": "mentioning"
        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.48550/arxiv.2606.14918",
          "targetDoi": "10.1103/physreve.87.043307",
          "snippet": "The tracers do not exert feedback on the fluid and do not interact directly with one another. The fluid velocity is interpolated to the particle positions using B-spline interpolation [119].",
          "section": "dns data",
          "type": "mentioning"
        }
      ],
      "isOa": true,
      "fulltextExcerpts": [
        "In this work, we introduce a Mori-Zwanzig graph neural network (MZ-GNN) for tracer dynamics in homogeneous isotropic turbulence. The method extends datadriven MZ modeling from single-particle Lagrangian dynamics to a genuine multi-particle setting.",
        "DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION\n\nIn this work, we have introduced a physics-structured surrogate model for multi-particle Lagrangian turbulence, combining the Mori-Zwanzig formalism [65,66,73,74] with equivariant graph neural networks [79,[83][84][85].",
        "We build on the regression-based data-driven Mori-Zwanzig framework developed in Refs. [73,74] and recently applied to single-particle Lagrangian turbulence in Ref. [77]. In this approach, the Markovian and memory operators of the Mori-Zwanzig expansion are learned from trajectory data, with regression defining an empirical projection onto the chosen resolved variables and function class."
